    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v9-iron-humidity-salts","source_ref":"source:candidate-v9-cci-care-iron","anchor":{"section":"Introduction"},"note":"CCI states bare iron rusts faster in humid air and faster still under a thin water film; water-soluble salts accelerate rust. Universal material relation only, not an historical maintenance custom, inventory, combat statistic, or scene condition.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v9-iron-oil-film","source_ref":"source:candidate-v9-cci-care-iron","anchor":{"section":"Caring for Stable Iron"},"note":"CCI says a thin oil film can temporarily protect underlying iron against rusting. Universal conservation relation only; no claim that a medieval weapon was oiled, maintained, usable, or currently present.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:candidate-v9-otrok-administrative-collector","source_ref":"source:candidate-v9-rsf-gippius-1144","anchor":{"section":"Грамота №1144"},"note":"The RSF summary reports Gippius’s reading of a complete late-XII-century Novgorod letter: in Novgorod documents otrok can denote an administrative person, a debt collector, rather than a child; the writer asks the price of his services. It is bounded documentary lexical/role context, not a general hierarchy, childhood category, office system, debt rule, or present NPC role.","review_status":"approved"},
